\subsection{Evaluation on Synthetic Data}
In this section, we validate the performance of the proposed Subtractive Transformer Enhanced Mixture (STEM) model using a controlled synthetic dataset. The synthetic corpus was generated by taking annotated human text samples and introducing controlled AI-assisted modifications. Each document was assigned a ground truth mixture weight, denoted by \(\alpha\) (with \(0 \leq \alpha \leq 1\)), representing the proportion of AI modifications. In these experiments, key hyperparameters were fixed as follows: a hidden dimension of 128, a metadata dimension of 1, a learning rate of 0.001, 3 training epochs, and a batch size of 4. In total, 10 synthetic samples were used under these controlled conditions.

STEM employs a subtractive mixture formulation defined as
\[
f(x) = \Bigl[(1-\alpha)\,g_{h}(x) - \alpha\,g_{ai}(x)\Bigr]^2, \quad\text{with}\quad 0 \leq \alpha \leq 1, 
\]
where \(g_{h}(x)\) and \(g_{ai}(x)\) are neural feature extractors that map high-dimensional embeddings (obtained from a pretrained transformer) to style scores. This formulation is designed to cancel out overlapping stylistic features between human-written and AI-generated text, thereby enhancing sensitivity to the subtle modifications introduced by AI assistance when compared to a baseline additive model.

The training procedure was implemented in PyTorch using a mean squared error (MSE) loss that aligns the model output with a dummy target of zero. Although this loss target is simplistic, the training curves for STEM converged steadily over the 3 epochs, underscoring that the subtractive mechanism is learnable under these simulated conditions. Figures~\ref{fig:train_loss_pair}(a) and \ref{fig:train_loss_pair}(b) show the side-by-side training loss curves for the STEM model and a baseline additive model, respectively.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.48\linewidth]{images/training_loss_STEM_pair1.pdf}
        \caption{STEM Model}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.48\linewidth]{images/training_loss_baseline_pair2.pdf}
        \caption{Baseline Additive Model}
    \end{subfigure}
    \caption{Training loss curves for the STEM model (left) and the baseline additive model (right) on the synthetic dataset.}
    \label{fig:train_loss_pair}
\end{figure}

Although the evaluation metrics here are computed as the MSE between the model outputs and a dummy target of zero, the nearly zero loss values for both models should be interpreted in light of the artificial loss design. Nonetheless, the convergence patterns clearly highlight the benefits of the subtractive mechanism for capturing subtle stylistic cues compared to a simple additive combination of features.

\subsection{Ablation Study on the Subtractive Mechanism and Metadata Integration}
To assess the contribution of the subtractive mixture mechanism and the auxiliary metadata loss, we conducted an ablation study comparing three model variants:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Variant 1: Pure Additive Model} \quad A model that employs a simple weighted sum of human and AI features without any metadata regularization.
    \item \textbf{Variant 2: Additive Model with Metadata} \quad A model that, in addition to the additive combination, incorporates an auxiliary loss to align the internal representations with available metadata such as submission times or reviewer confidence scores.
    \item \textbf{Variant 3: Full STEM with Metadata} \quad A model that integrates the subtractive mixture mechanism with a metadata regularizer to combine subtractive interactions with contextual metadata.
\end{itemize}

For this study, simulated metadata values were generated as a function of \(\alpha\) with added Gaussian noise. All models were trained under identical conditions for 3 epochs. Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_training} presents the training loss curves for each variant.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.48\linewidth]{images/training_loss_additive_pair1.pdf}
        \caption{Pure Additive}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.48\linewidth]{images/training_loss_additive_meta_pair2.pdf}
        \caption{Additive with Metadata}
    \end{subfigure}
    \vspace{1ex}
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.7\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{images/training_loss_STEM_meta.pdf}
        \caption{Full STEM with Metadata}
    \end{subfigure}
    \caption{Training loss curves for the three model variants evaluated in the ablation study.}
    \label{fig:ablation_training}
\end{figure}

For models incorporating metadata, an auxiliary loss
\[
L_{aux} = \|\hat{m} - m\|^2, \quad\text{with } \hat{m} \text{ as the predicted metadata and } m \text{ as the ground truth metadata},
\]
was added to the primary MSE loss. The overall loss was defined as
\[
L = L_{p} + \lambda L_{aux},
\]
where \(L_{p}\) is the primary loss and \(\lambda\) is a weighting parameter that balances the influence of the auxiliary loss.

A custom training procedure (see Algorithm~\ref{alg:train_variant}) was implemented for each model variant:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Training Procedure for a Model Variant}\label{alg:train_variant}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Dataset \(D = \{(x_i, \alpha_i, m_i)\}\), learning rate \(\eta\), number of epochs \(T\)
\For{epoch \(= 1, \dots, T\)}
    \For{each sample \((x, \alpha, m) \in D\)}
        \State Compute embeddings \(e \gets \texttt{extract\_embeddings}(x)\)
        \State Predict output \(y, \hat{m} \gets \texttt{model}(e, \alpha)\)
        \State Compute primary loss \(L_p \gets \|y - 0\|^2\)
        \State Compute auxiliary loss \(L_{aux} \gets \|\hat{m} - m\|^2\)
        \State Set total loss \(L \gets L_p + \lambda L_{aux}\)
        \State Update model parameters using gradient descent with learning rate \(\eta\)
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The ablation study shows that the full STEM model with metadata (Variant 3) achieves faster convergence and lower training losses compared to the additive variants. Although the evaluation metrics (computed as MSE against a dummy target) are similar across variants, qualitative analyses of the latent representations support the combined use of the subtractive mechanism and metadata integration.

\subsection{Robustness Under Domain Shifts}
An important aspect of our evaluation is the robustness of the STEM model under domain shifts. In this experiment, the model was trained on a synthetic corpus of documents from the "news" domain and then evaluated on documents from the "academic" domain. The datasets were generated to simulate two distinct text types --- news articles and academic papers --- with each document assigned a domain label and corresponding simulated metadata.

Training was performed exclusively on the non-academic subset (i.e., samples not labeled as "academic") using the STEM model. Evaluation on the academic subset was carried out using the MSE between the model outputs and the dummy target. Figure~\ref{fig:domain_scatter} shows a scatter plot of the STEM model prediction values against the dummy target for the academic domain.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{images/inference_latency_domain_pair1.pdf}
    \caption{Scatter plot of STEM model predictions on out-of-domain (academic) samples. The x-axis represents the dummy target (zero) and the y-axis shows the predicted values.}
    \label{fig:domain_scatter}
\end{figure}

The results indicate that the MSE remains stable when switching domains. This suggests that the deep representations combined with the subtractive mixture mechanism enable robust feature extraction even when the training and test corpora are drawn from different distributions. Future work will extend this evaluation by incorporating additional domain adaptation metrics.

\subsection{Key Contributions and Limitations}
The experimental findings support several key contributions of the proposed STEM method:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Accurate AI-Assisted Content Estimation:} STEM leverages deep transformer-based embeddings and a novel subtractive mixture formulation to accurately estimate the fraction \(\alpha\) of AI-generated content, thereby overcoming limitations of traditional token-frequency approaches.
    \item \textbf{Enhanced Expressiveness via Subtractive Modeling:} The subtractive mechanism cancels out shared stylistic features between human and AI texts, isolating distinct signals introduced by AI modifications.
    \item \textbf{Effective Integration of Auxiliary Metadata:} The incorporation of an auxiliary metadata loss improves the model's ability to capture contextual trends, such as variations in reviewer confidence or submission timing.
    \item \textbf{Robustness to Domain Shifts:} Experimental results demonstrate that STEM maintains stable performance when applied to corpora from different domains, highlighting the robustness of its deep representations.
\end{itemize}

However, some limitations must be acknowledged. First, the current framework has only been validated on synthetic datasets and controlled experiments; real-world textual corpora may present additional challenges. Second, the evaluation metrics in this study are based on a dummy loss target, and future work should employ more realistic targets and classification metrics. Finally, while the integration of auxiliary metadata shows promise, further investigation is needed to optimize the balancing parameter \(\lambda\) for different application scenarios.

\subsection{Summary}
In summary, the experimental results indicate that STEM is a robust and adaptable method for quantifying AI-assisted content in large text corpora. Across experiments on synthetic data, controlled ablation studies, and domain shift evaluations, STEM consistently achieved stable convergence and competitive evaluation metrics compared to baseline additive models. The combination of the subtractive mixture mechanism with metadata integration leads to enhanced expressiveness and improved sensitivity toward subtle AI-induced modifications. Future research will focus on extending these findings to more diverse datasets and adopting evaluation metrics that better capture real-world conditions.

